WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.960
It can actually be pretty tough to get actors
to share their true feelings about their films,

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.680
especially if they're the films they'd rather
not talk about.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:12.420
Fortunately, a handful of stars have opened
up over the years, and taken aim at the worst

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:13.500
movies of their career.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:19.520
In 2002, Halle Berry delivered a tearful,
impassioned speech on the Oscars stage, clutching

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.560
the Best Actress statuette she'd just earned
for her performance in Monster's Ball.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:28.200
Three years later, she brought that same statuette
with her to accept her Razzie Award for Worst

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.700
Actress in the super-zero flop Catwoman.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.880
Berry delighted the audience with her snide
speech, in which she thanked studio Warner

00:00:34.889 --> 00:00:38.100
Brothers for putting her in what she called
a "god-awful movie."

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.309
Berry went on to say:

00:00:39.309 --> 00:00:40.940
"It was just what my career needed.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:44.940
You know, I was at the top, and then Catwoman
has plummeted me to the bottom."

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.179
Although her Catwoman speech has since gone
down in Hollywood legend, Berry's attitude

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.500
towards the movie has mellowed in recent years.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:56.700
In a 2018 acceptance speech, this time for
the Matrix Award for New York Women in Communication,

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.500
Berry said of her Razzie-winning role:

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.179
"While it failed to most people, it wasn't
a failure for me because I met so many interesting

00:01:03.179 --> 00:01:07.281
people that I wouldn't have met otherwise,
I learned two forms of martial arts, and I

00:01:07.281 --> 00:01:08.580
learned not what to do."

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:12.120
It also helped, she added, that the film made
her a life-changing amount of money.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:17.720
Dev Patel found stardom early in his career
with the Oscar-winning Slumdog Millionaire.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:22.740
So naturally, everyone was pretty curious
about his next film: 2010's Avatar: The Last

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:23.240
Airbender.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:27.780
It was a huge studio movie with huge expectations
and an even bigger budget.

00:01:27.789 --> 00:01:32.619
Of course, once it hit theaters, the movie
became hated by pretty much everyone on Earth,

00:01:32.619 --> 00:01:35.320
and for a while, Patel didn't really want
to talk about it.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:38.030
Back in 2014, the most he said on the matter
was:

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.030
"Yeah, that wasn't the best move, was it?"

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:44.640
Patel opened up a little further in a 2016
roundtable interview with The Hollywood Reporter.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.780
"I completely felt overwhelmed by the experience
I felt like I wasn't being heard."

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:53.650
Still, Patel pointed out that making the movie
had been a learning experience for him.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.920
"That's really when I learned the power of
no.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:01.760
The idea of saying no, because I was like
18 and was just like, 'okay.'"

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.420
Harry Potter may have made Daniel Radcliffe
a household name, but that still doesn't mean

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.140
he's fond of every film in the series.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:12.620
In 2014, Radcliffe told the Daily Mail that
his favorite of the franchise is Order of

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:16.459
the Phoenix, and that the sixth movie, The
Half-Blood Prince, was his least favorite

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:17.120
of the bunch.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:21.120
In 2015, Playboy asked Radcliffe to expand
on this, and he explained:

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:25.100
"In every movie up to the sixth one, you can
see a big step forward in my acting.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.140
And then it stopped, or went backward maybe,
in the sixth film.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:32.200
[...] On the sixth, I remember watching it
and thinking, 'Wow, there's been no growth.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.340
You're watching a mistake you made every day
for 11 months.'

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.400
That's the way I saw it."

00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:40.520
It probably didn't help that Radcliffe had
started drinking heavily when he turned 18,

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.819
the age at which he filmed The Half-Blood
Prince.

00:02:42.819 --> 00:02:46.880
Radcliffe didn't explicitly mention this when
discussing his least favorite of the films,

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:50.420
but the actor had previously spoken about
his alcohol abuse in 2012.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:54.230
He said that while he never drank on set,
he would still be drunk from the night before,

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:55.870
and that watching the movies now:

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.100
"I can point to many scenes where I'm just
gone.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:59.380
Dead behind the eyes."

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.140
Luckily, Radcliffe became a teetotaler in
2010.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:08.560
In 2012, when a 3D version of Titanic docked
in theaters for the film's 15th anniversary,

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:10.019
Kate Winslet told CNN:

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:13.100
"Every single scene, I'm like 'Really, really?

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.100
You did it like that?

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:15.000
Oh my God.'

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:19.230
[...] I have a hard time watching any of my
performances, but watching Titanic I was just

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.230
like, 'Oh God, I want to do that again.'"

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:25.040
It might be surprising to hear that Winslet
wants to give her performance as Rose a second

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.520
go, given what she went through the first
time around.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.220
Director James Cameron was notoriously aggressive
on set, , Winslet told the LA Times:

00:03:32.239 --> 00:03:34.939
"There were times when I was genuinely frightened
of him."

00:03:34.939 --> 00:03:39.070
On top of that, Winslet nearly drowned while
shooting one scene, and Cameron made her do

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:43.469
it again right after and has since dismissed
her claim that she was in real danger.

00:03:43.469 --> 00:03:48.030
She also chipped a bone, came out with various
bruises and scrapes, and was regularly made

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:49.340
to perform in cold water.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:54.100
For close-ups, she was even submerged 12 feet
underwater and weighted down so she wouldn't

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:54.600
move.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.140
No wonder she doesn't like watching it.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:01.160
In recent years, actors as prestigious as
Robert Redford and Anthony Hopkins have taken

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.200
major roles in superhero films.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.829
But there's one two-time Oscar winner who
seriously regrets joining the world of comic

00:04:06.829 --> 00:04:07.520
book movies.

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:12.260
As it turns out, Sally Field though her part
as Aunt May in the Amazing Spider-Man movies

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:13.629
was an absolute bore.

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:17.700
In an appearance on The Howard Stern Show,
Field talked in depth about Sony's failed

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:18.820
attempt at a trilogy.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:19.740
"Spider-Man!

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:21.220
You didn’t like that movie?

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:24.800
“Ah not, not especially, it’s not my kind
of movie."

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:29.240
She also added that she did it for her friend,
Amazing Spider-Man producer Laura Ziskin,

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.480
who died a year before the film was released.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:35.250
But Field also admitted that she didn't put
a great deal of thought into the character.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:41.250
"You work it as much as you can, you know,
but you can’t put 10 pounds of s--- in a

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:41.880
five pound bag.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:46.380
Still, Field managed to find a bright side
in all the mess according to the actress,

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.840
working with Andrew Garfield was at least
a lot of fun.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:54.160
In 2010, Jessica Alba told Elle magazine that
shooting Fantastic Four: Rise of the Silver

00:04:54.160 --> 00:05:00.010
Surfer nearly made her quit acting, as director
Tim Story apparently made Alba doubt her abilities

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:01.000
and her career choice.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:05.300
Evidently, Story didn't like the way she was
crying in a scene because it looked "too real."

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.830
According to Alba, the director then asked
her to "cry pretty."

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:09.830
As Alba put it:

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:12.200
"And then it all got me thinking: Am I not
good enough?

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.680
Are my instincts and my emotions not good
enough?

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.600
Do people hate them so much that they don't
want me to be a person?

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:18.840
[…] And so I just said, 'F--- it.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:21.240
I don't care about this business anymore.'"

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:22.760
Alba was as good as her word.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:27.860
In 2012, she traded Hollywood for business,
setting up The Honest Company, which focuses

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:29.740
on selling natural baby products.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:34.400
According to Forbes, that first year, her
company made $10 million in revenue.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:39.530
Alba then expanded to beauty products in 2015,
and she raised $200 million in funding in

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:40.160
2018.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:44.740
And although Alba is still acting today, the
fact that Forbes estimates her company to

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:48.460
be worth $200 million gives her an out if
she ever changes her mind.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:53.540
Since the rise and demise of his relationship with Angelina Jolie, you could make the case

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:57.620
that Brad Pitt has become more famous for
his personal life than for his movie career.

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:02.140
But back in the '90s, Brad Pitt was a bona
fide movie star although he still occasionally

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:05.690
had some pretty blunt comments to make about
the movies that made him a household name.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:10.680
For example, in 1997, Pitt called out The
Devil's Own, in which he starred as an undercover

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:13.100
IRA terrorist opposite Harrison Ford.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.100
Pitt told Newsweek:

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:15.300
"We had no script.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:18.120
Well, we had a great script, but it got tossed
for various reasons."

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:21.680
According to Pitt, the cast and crew were
forced "to make something up" as they went

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:26.390
along, a move that Pitt called the most irresponsible
bit of filmmaking that he had ever seen.

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:31.680
He also called out then-head of Columbia studios,
Mark Canton, for forcing production to continue.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:35.480
Pitt apparently regretted his comments shortly
afterwards, however, adding in a follow-up

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:35.980
interview:

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:37.300
"Everyone was working hard.

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:39.540
We weren't trying to just throw out a crap
film.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:40.900
You know, we were doing our best."

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:46.000
The Indiana Jones series is nearly a perfect
franchise with the exception of Kingdom of

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:47.140
the Crystal Skull, that is.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:51.360
Although to be fair, Crystal Skull actually
did pretty well when it came out in 2008,

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:52.860
at least box office-wise.

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:57.440
But in the years since, it's come under severe
scrutiny for its supposed betrayal of the

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:59.080
classic Indiana Jones spirit.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:04.000
In fact, Shia LaBeouf, who played a motorcycle
punk named Mutt, said that both he and director

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:06.820
Steven Spielberg had "dropped the ball" on
the movie.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:11.300
In 2018, he went back on the attack again,
this time taking aim at the oft-mocked, Michael

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:14.490
Bay-directed Transformers franchise that made
him a star.

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:15.670
LeBeouf told Esquire:

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:18.480
"My hang-up with those films was that they
felt irrelevant.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:23.000
[...] You come up on these stories about Easy
Rider and Raging Bull and De Niro and Scorsese

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.320
and Hopper, and you find value in what they
do.

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:27.660
Meanwhile, you're chasing energon crystals."

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:32.260
After getting his fill of blockbusters, LaBeouf
moved toward performance art but many still

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:34.720
remember him as a guy who bit the hand that
fed him.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:38.840
Long before he took the role of the Caped
Crusader, Robert Pattinson launched his career

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:40.820
with a batty role of another kind.

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:45.880
Quite notoriously, Pattinson got his big break
playing a vampire in the Twilight series although

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:48.240
Edward Cullen was more sparkly than sinister.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.780
Based on a series of YA novels, the Twilight
franchise was loved and loathed with almost

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:57.300
equal passion… and Pattinson found himself
firmly on the latter side of the equation.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:01.720
In interviews during the saga's many press
tours, his comments tended to range from anywhere

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:02.900
between bewildered...

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:05.470
"A lot of stuff in the Twilight world that
doesn’t make any sense."

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:06.580
...to frustrated...

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:08.020
"I hardly get to do any vampire stuff.

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:13.210
I mean, I really don’t get to kill anyone.

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:14.210
really wanna kill some people."

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:15.200
...to outright belligerent.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:21.900
"The story's just so serious or so melodramatic…

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:24.340
I mean it’s literally like life or death
all the time."

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:29.300
Since the final installment came out in 2012,
Pattinson has mostly stuck to more artistic

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:29.800
movies.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:33.840
However, he's managed to find a few positive
things about Twilight since then, admitting

00:08:33.849 --> 00:08:37.959
that since the intensity around the films
has died down, he has learned to appreciate

00:08:37.959 --> 00:08:39.829
fans' enjoyment of it far more.

00:08:39.829 --> 00:08:43.229
He even found one part of the film that he
enjoyed, telling USA Today:

00:08:43.229 --> 00:08:46.000
"[New Moon] genuinely does have an incredible
good soundtrack.

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:49.160
I completely forgot, but the soundtracks were
quite ahead of their time."

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:54.280
Jamie Lee Curtis impressed fans and critics
alike when she returned to Haddonfield in

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:55.340
2018's Halloween.

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:59.779
But even though Curtis has also starred in
classics like A Fish Called Wanda and True

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:02.440
Lies, her Hollywood record isn't exactly spotless.

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:07.240
According to Curtis, the worst movie she ever
made was Virus, a movie about a tugboat crew

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:11.339
that stumbles upon an almost abandoned ship
before things get all kinds of crazy.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:15.759
While the premise might sound awesome, critics
hated the film, and Curtis clearly wasn't

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:16.760
happy with it, either.

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:21.960
In 2012, she told IGN that when she and her
movie star friends compare bad movies, Virus

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:22.990
is her trump card.

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:23.500
She explained:

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:25.820
"That's the only good reason to be in bad
movies.

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:29.680
Then when your friends have [bad] movies you
can say 'Ahhhh, I've got the best one.'

00:09:29.689 --> 00:09:30.740
I'm bringing Virus."

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:35.110
When you've got a career as long and varied
as Michelle Pfeiffer's, you're bound to have

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:36.940
starred in a dud every now and again.

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:41.280
Luckily, Pfeiffer's low point came at the
very start of her career and it probably would've

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:44.920
been forgotten if it hadn't also happened
to be a sequel to one of the most beloved

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:46.080
movies of all time.

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:50.720
Pfeiffer starred as Pink Lady Stephanie in
Grease 2, four years after the original movie

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:51.820
musical came out.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:56.180
After many misfires during production, the
movie barely made back its budget.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:58.339
In 2007, she told MovieWeb:

00:09:58.339 --> 00:10:01.870
"I hated that film with a vengeance and could
not believe how bad it was.

00:10:01.870 --> 00:10:04.360
At the time, I was young and didn't know any
better.

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:05.960
I hear it's a cult movie now."

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:10.500
Thankfully, her next film was Scarface which
did a little better but Pfeiffer admits she

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:12.200
wouldn't mind going back to Rydell High.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:15.840
Discussing the prospect of another Grease
movie, she explained to MovieWeb:

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:18.069
"I would love to play the school principal.

00:10:18.069 --> 00:10:20.220
She is fun and has some great put-down lines."

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:25.160
Ben Affleck already had a long list of dubious
movies under his belt before he strapped on

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:30.380
the utility belt to play Batman in Zack Snyder's
much maligned Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice.

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:34.060
But of all his awful films, Affleck hates
Daredevil the most.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:38.880
This 2003 adaptation of the Marvel comic is
despised by every human being who's ever stepped

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:43.120
inside a movie theater but the man who loathes
it the most is definitely its leading man.

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:47.420
Before the release of Batman v Superman, Affleck
explained that part of the reason he wanted

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:51.140
to play a superhero again was because he wanted
a second chance at the genre.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:53.399
During his appearance at TimeTalks, he explained:

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:57.740
"Part of it was I wanted for once to get one
of these movies and do it right to do a good

00:10:57.740 --> 00:10:58.240
version.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:10:59.960
I hate Daredevil so much."

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:04.620
Unfortunately, his shot at the Batman franchise
didn't quite deliver the redemption he'd wished

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:08.940
for, as the movie was widely panned by critics
— but hey, it could have been worse.

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:14.480
"I missed."

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:19.960
In 2018, when The New York Times asked Davis
if there was a job she wished she'd taken,

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:23.779
Viola Davis reworked the question to dish
on a job she wished she'd turned down and

00:11:23.779 --> 00:11:28.529
that was the role of Aibileen, a black maid
working in 1960s Mississippi who joins the

00:11:28.529 --> 00:11:30.960
Civil Rights Movement in 2011's The Help.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:35.240
Davis was quick to praise her co-stars and
director Tate Taylor before getting to the

00:11:35.249 --> 00:11:36.700
reason behind her regret.

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:37.440
As she put it:

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:41.040
"I just felt that at the end of the day, that
it wasn't the voices of the maids that were

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:41.540
heard."

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:45.520
According to Davis, the film, which was supposed
to be about black women working and raising

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:49.680
children in 1963, didn't really put their
stories front and center.

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:53.440
Davis was actually nominated for an Oscar
for the role, but there's a good chance she'd

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:54.980
rather just forget it ever happened.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:59.839
Alec Baldwin isn't exactly known for holding
back his opinions, and that goes just the

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:01.380
same when it comes to his own work.

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:06.579
One of the films he's taken aim at is 2012's
Rock of Ages, a movie based on a stage musical

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:10.080
that features a loose plot constructed around
covers of classic rock songs.

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:14.370
The movie failed to rock critics' worlds,
and no one was less surprised than Baldwin

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:17.829
who admitted that those co-stars were why
he'd signed up in the first place.

00:12:17.829 --> 00:12:21.129
In 2013, Baldwin told New Yorker editor David
Remnick:

00:12:21.129 --> 00:12:22.759
"It was a complete disaster.

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:25.040
A week in you go, 'Oh God, what have I done?'"

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:30.240
Indeed, in 2011, before filming had even begun,
Baldwin asked New Line to replace him.

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:34.660
Supposedly this was due to an unspecified
medical condition, but given that Baldwin

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:38.500
thinks he made what he called "a horrible
movie," it could well have been a premonition

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:39.400
of the flop to come.

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:43.720
This theory seems especially valid, given
that Baldwin also claimed actors know full

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:45.740
well when they have a dud on their hands.

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:46.360
He explained:

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:47.580
"The plane is buffeting.

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:48.820
The engine is on fire."

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:51.920
Millions of kids may have grown up wanting
to be G.I.

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:55.910
Joe, but when Channing Tatum got the chance,
he was actually less than thrilled.

00:12:55.910 --> 00:12:58.009
In 2015, six years after G.I.

00:12:58.009 --> 00:13:02.870
Joe: The Rise of Cobra came out, Tatum told
Howard Stern that, after receiving the script,

00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:05.800
he found himself a little less than thrilled
at the idea of taking the role.

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:11.040
"But do you have no option to no option, or
do they say you’re doing this or we're gonna

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:11.540
sue you."

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:15.400
He also admitted to being wary of the role
in the first place, because he'd been such

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:17.160
a fan of the show during his youth.

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:22.610
"I don't wanna do something that one I’m
a fan of, like I watched it every morning

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:23.610
growing up."

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:28.079
He explained to Stern that, after his first
movie role in 2005's Coach Carter, he signed

00:13:28.079 --> 00:13:29.879
a three-picture deal with Paramount.

00:13:29.879 --> 00:13:31.649
They cashed in part of that deal with G.I.

00:13:31.649 --> 00:13:35.660
Joe, and informed Tatum that they would sue
him if he refused to make the film.

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:41.040
Luckily, Tatum's career recovered and in 2015,
he was the 13th highest-paid actor in the

00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:41.560
world.

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:42.140
But G.I.

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:46.540
Joe wasn't done with him after Cobra: perhaps
unfortunately for Tatum, he also went on to

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:48.619
appear in the 2013 sequel G.I.

00:13:48.619 --> 00:13:49.889
Joe: Retaliation.

00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:54.670
While he may have found superstardom with
roles in classic movies like The Terminator

00:13:54.670 --> 00:13:58.500
and Predator, Arnold Schwazenegger still starred
in a few duds in his time.

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:03.399
In 1985, Arnie appeared in Red Sonja, which
was supposed to be the story of a vengeful

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:06.120
female warrior even though Arnie got top billing.

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:10.240
Confusingly, although the character of Red
Sonja originally appeared as a sidekick for

00:14:10.250 --> 00:14:14.470
Conan the Barbarian in Marvel comics, and
Schwarzenegger had already played Conan twice

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:19.070
by this point, Red Sonja finds actor playing
a totally different sword-wielding, muscle-bound

00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:20.600
warrior named Kalidor.

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:23.900
Unfortunately, Schwarzenegger's performance
was less than charming.

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:27.940
The New York Times pointed out that his character,
quote, "might as well have been played by

00:14:27.949 --> 00:14:30.880
a maple tree", and Schwarzenegger seems to
agree.

00:14:30.880 --> 00:14:32.240
He once said of the flick:

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:34.240
"It's the worst film I have ever made.

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:37.660
Now when my kids get out of line, they're
sent to their room and forced to watch Red

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:39.009
Sonja ten times.

00:14:39.009 --> 00:14:40.820
I never have too much trouble with them."

